WEBVTT

00:00.080 --> 00:00.400
All right.

00:00.440 --> 00:03.400
So now the big question is how to build the bronze layer.

00:03.440 --> 00:05.840
So first things first we do analizing.

00:05.880 --> 00:09.440
As you are developing anything you don't immediately start writing a code.

00:09.440 --> 00:14.760
So before we start coding the bronze layer, what we usually do is we have to understand the source

00:14.760 --> 00:15.320
system.

00:15.320 --> 00:21.200
So what I usually do, I make an interview with the source system experts and ask them many, many questions

00:21.200 --> 00:26.760
in order to understand the nature of the source system that I'm connecting to the data warehouse.

00:26.760 --> 00:30.160
And once you know the source systems, then we can start coding.

00:30.160 --> 00:33.160
And the main focus here is to do the data ingestion.

00:33.160 --> 00:39.080
So that means we have to find a way on how to load the data from the source into the data warehouse.

00:39.080 --> 00:44.200
So it's like we are building a bridge between the source and our target system, the data warehouse.

00:44.200 --> 00:48.560
And once we have the code ready, the next step is to have to do data validation.

00:48.560 --> 00:50.440
So here comes the quality control.

00:50.440 --> 00:53.920
It is very important in the bronze layer to check the data completeness.

00:53.920 --> 01:00.280
So that means we have to compare the number of records between the source system and the bronze layer,

01:00.320 --> 01:02.760
just to make sure we are not losing any data in between.

01:02.760 --> 01:07.300
And another check that we will be doing is the schema checks, and that's to make sure that the data

01:07.300 --> 01:09.300
is placed on the right position.

01:09.300 --> 01:14.540
And finally, we don't have to forget about documentation and committing our work in the git.

01:14.580 --> 01:18.140
So this is the process that we're going to follow to build the bronze layer.

01:18.300 --> 01:19.140
All right my friends.

01:19.140 --> 01:23.620
So now before connecting any source systems to our data warehouse, we have to make a very important

01:23.620 --> 01:26.180
step is to understand the sources.

01:26.180 --> 01:31.460
So how I usually do it I set up a meeting with the source systems experts in order to interview them,

01:31.460 --> 01:33.940
to ask them a lot of stuff about the source.

01:33.980 --> 01:39.220
And gaining this knowledge is very important, because asking the right question will help you to design

01:39.220 --> 01:44.500
the correct scripts in order to extract the data and to avoid a lot of mistakes and challenges.

01:44.540 --> 01:49.100
And now I'm going to show you the most common questions that I usually ask before connecting anything.

01:49.140 --> 01:49.380
Okay.

01:49.420 --> 01:53.420
So we start first by understanding the business context and the ownership.

01:53.420 --> 01:56.020
So I would like to understand the story behind the data.

01:56.060 --> 02:00.300
I would like to understand who is responsible for the data, which IT departments and so on.

02:00.300 --> 02:04.580
And then it's nice to understand as well what business process it supports.

02:04.580 --> 02:10.520
Does it support the customer transactions, the supply chain logistics or maybe finance reporting.

02:10.600 --> 02:13.160
So with that you can understand the importance of your data.

02:13.200 --> 02:16.560
And then I ask about the system and data documentation.

02:16.560 --> 02:20.960
So having documentations from the source is your learning materials about your data.

02:20.960 --> 02:26.480
And it saves you a lot of time later when you are working and designing maybe new data models.

02:26.480 --> 02:31.080
And as well I would like always to understand the data model for the source system.

02:31.080 --> 02:35.280
And if they have like descriptions of the columns and the tables, it's going to be nice to have the

02:35.280 --> 02:36.000
data catalog.

02:36.000 --> 02:40.480
This can helps me a lot in the data warehouse, how I'm going to go and join the tables together.

02:40.480 --> 02:45.840
So with that you get a solid foundations about the business context, the processes and the ownership

02:45.840 --> 02:46.480
of the data.

02:46.520 --> 02:50.000
And now in the next step we're going to start talking about the technicality.

02:50.000 --> 02:53.600
So I would like to understand the architecture and as well the technology stack.

02:53.600 --> 02:58.360
So the first question that I usually ask is how the source system is storing the data.

02:58.360 --> 03:04.160
Do we have the data on the on prem like in SQL Server or Oracle, or is it in the cloud like Azure,

03:04.600 --> 03:05.520
AWS and so on.

03:05.560 --> 03:11.150
And then once we understand that, then we can discuss what are the integration capabilities, like

03:11.150 --> 03:12.790
how I'm gonna go and get the data.

03:12.790 --> 03:18.630
Do the source system offer APIs, maybe Kafka, or they have only like file extractions, or they're

03:18.630 --> 03:21.630
gonna give you, like a direct connection to the database.

03:21.630 --> 03:25.910
So once you understand the technology that you're going to use in order to extract the data, then we're

03:25.910 --> 03:28.390
gonna deep dive into more technical questions.

03:28.390 --> 03:33.590
And here we can understand how to extract the data from the source system and then load it into the

03:33.590 --> 03:34.590
data warehouse.

03:34.590 --> 03:39.790
So the first thing that we have to discuss with the experts, can we do an incremental load or a full

03:39.790 --> 03:40.150
load.

03:40.190 --> 03:44.310
And then after that we're going to discuss the data scope the sterilizations.

03:44.310 --> 03:45.590
Do we need all data.

03:45.590 --> 03:47.870
Do we need only maybe ten years of the data?

03:47.950 --> 03:53.430
Are there histories already in the source system or should we build it in the data warehouse and so

03:53.430 --> 03:53.750
on.

03:53.790 --> 03:57.990
And then we're going to go and discuss what is the expected size of the extracts.

03:58.030 --> 04:01.510
Are we talking here about megabytes gigabytes, terabytes.

04:01.550 --> 04:07.150
And this is very important to understand whether we have the right tools and platform to connect the

04:07.150 --> 04:07.870
source system.

04:07.910 --> 04:12.590
And then I try to understand whether there are any data volume limitations, like if you have some old

04:12.590 --> 04:16.250
source systems they might struggle a lot with performance and so on.

04:16.250 --> 04:21.690
So if you have like an ETL that is extracting large amounts of data, you might bring the performance

04:21.690 --> 04:23.050
down of that source system.

04:23.050 --> 04:27.610
So that's why you have to try to understand whether there are any limitations for your extracts, and

04:27.610 --> 04:31.610
as well other aspects that might impact the performance of the source system.

04:31.610 --> 04:32.530
This is very important.

04:32.530 --> 04:37.650
If they give you an access to the database, you have to be responsible that you are not bringing the

04:37.650 --> 04:39.810
performance of the database down.

04:39.810 --> 04:44.730
And of course, very important question is to ask about the authentication and the authorization, like

04:44.730 --> 04:47.370
how are you going to go and access the data in the source system?

04:47.370 --> 04:50.410
Do you need any tokens, keys, passwords and so on.

04:50.410 --> 04:55.450
So those are the questions that you have to ask if you are connecting new source system to the data

04:55.450 --> 04:56.130
warehouse.

04:56.130 --> 05:01.090
And once you have the answers for those questions, you can proceed with the next steps to connect the

05:01.090 --> 05:03.050
sources to the data warehouse.

05:03.090 --> 05:03.850
All right, my friends.

05:03.850 --> 05:08.770
So with that, you have learned how to analyze new source systems that you want to connect to your data

05:08.770 --> 05:09.370
warehouse.

05:09.370 --> 05:10.770
So this tip is done.

05:10.770 --> 05:15.250
And now we're going to go back to coding where we're going to write scripts in order to do the data

05:15.250 --> 05:18.210
ingestion from the CSV files to the process layer.
